## TuffEx Engineering Implementation

TuffEx draws superellipse corners with CSS `corner-shape`: `border-radius` sets how much of each edge the corner takes, and `superellipse(K)` has the exponent $n = 2^K$. `tx-squircle` grows the radius to $(1 + s) \cdot R$, then picks the exponent that cuts as deep along the 45° diagonal as a circular arc of radius R. The default $s = 0.6$ gives $1.6R$ and `superellipse(1.777)` ($n \approx 3.43$):

:::TuffCodeBlock{lang="scss"}
---
code: |
  // packages/tuffex/packages/components/style/mixins.scss
  @mixin tx-squircle($radius: 16px, $smoothness: $tx-squircle-smoothness, $inset: 0) {
    border-radius: tx-squircle-inset($radius, $inset);
    @supports (corner-shape: superellipse(2)) {
      border-radius: tx-squircle-inset(tx-squircle-radius($radius, $smoothness), $inset);
      corner-shape: superellipse(#{tx-squircle-exponent($smoothness)});
    }
  }
---
:::

- **Fallback**: browsers without `corner-shape` (Safari, Firefox) keep the plain radius. TuffEx does not reshape them with an SVG mask: a mask clips the outer shadow and leaves a `backdrop-filter` inside nothing to sample, which breaks the default refraction material.
- **Tokens and utility classes**: code that cannot include the mixin reads `--tx-corner-smooth-scale` and `--tx-corner-smooth-shape`; templates can use `.tx-corner-smooth-sm`, `-md` and `-lg` (8 / 16 / 24px).
- **Child layers**: a layer that copies the outline with `border-radius: inherit` also needs `corner-shape: inherit`, because the property does not inherit. A layer that sits inside a border or padding cannot inherit at all: pass that distance to `tx-squircle` as `$inset`, which takes it off the radius and keeps the layer concentric with its host.

TxCard, TxModal, TxCommandPalette, TxFlipOverlay and the dialogs already use smooth corners, as does CoreBox with its rounded appearance, so refracted edges follow one continuous curve.
